  "name": "Example: Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical",
  "problem": "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x-6} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2x-6})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2x-6 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(5)-6} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$  is $x=5.$",
